  "omissions": []
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "status": "supported",
      "witness": null,
      "reason": "The claimed probability (9.6%) is a sensible rounding of the evidence's calibrated_pd of 0.0959 (9.59%). The horizon ('payment due after the September 2005 statement') matches the evidence's target_definition and horizon."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "status": "supported",
      "witness": null,
      "reason": "The repayment status in September 2005 (PAY_0, f1) has attribution a1 with contribution -0.5795, which is the largest absolute contribution among all attributions (|−0.5795| > |−0.5181|, |−0.4597|, |0.4259|). The code -2 is correctly noted as undocumented. The direction 'decreases_risk' matches the negative contribution. No violation of R_VALUE, R_DIRECTION, R_RANKING, or R_INVENTED."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "status": "supported",
      "witness": null,
      "reason": "The claim lists three factors: (1) max repayment status (pay_max, a2, contrib -0.5181), (2) credit amount (LIMIT_BAL, a3, contrib -0.4597), and (3) amount paid in September (PAY_AMT1, a4, contrib +0.4259). All are correctly named, valued, and their directional effects (decreases/increases risk) are accurately described. The phrase 'which is 0.0 NT dollars and increases the risk score' correctly refers only to PAY_AMT1, not the others. The claim does not assert causation, invent factors, or misstate scales. The 'why_not_checked' note about mixed directions is not a violation — the claim correctly attributes direction per factor."
